        <h3>Step 3: Pass CCSE Culture Test — Spanish Law & Society</h3>
        <p>The CCSE (Conocimiento Constitucional y Cívico de España, or Constitutional and Civic Knowledge of Spain) test assesses your understanding of Spanish government, law, history, and values. It ensures that new citizens understand the constitutional framework and core values they are pledging to uphold.</p>
        <p><strong>What the test covers:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>The Spanish Constitution and structure of government (parliament, monarchy, judicial system)</li>
          <li>Citizen rights and obligations</li>
          <li>Regional organization and autonomous communities</li>
          <li>Spanish history and cultural heritage</li>
          <li>Values of democracy and rule of law</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Test format:</strong> 25 multiple-choice questions. You must answer at least 15 correctly to pass. The test is administered in Spanish and typically lasts 45 minutes.</p>
        <p><strong>Cost and availability:</strong> The CCSE costs approximately €30 and is offered regularly through official examination centers. Study materials and sample tests are provided by the Spanish Administration.</p>
        <p><strong>Preparation strategy:</strong> Most applicants prepare for 4–8 weeks using official study guides. The test is straightforward if you familiarize yourself with Spanish constitutional basics and current government structures.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 4: Gather Documents -->
      <div style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
        <h3>Step 4: Gather Documents — Complete Your Application File</h3>
        <p>Document collection is critical. A missing or incorrectly certified document can delay your application by months or result in rejection. Begin gathering documents early—some require time to obtain or translate.</p>
        <p>See our <strong>Documents You'll Need</strong> section below for a complete checklist and specific requirements.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 5: Submit Application Online -->
      <div style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
        <h3>Step 5: Submit Application Online — Via Ministry of Justice Portal</h3>
        <p>All citizenship applications are filed through the official Ministry of Justice electronic platform at <strong>sede.mjusticia.gob.es</strong>. You will need a valid digital certificate (certificado digital) or Spanish electronic ID (DNI electrónico) to authenticate your submission.</p>
        <p><strong>What you submit:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Completed application form (Solicitud de Nacionalidad)</li>
          <li>Proof of DELE A2 and CCSE exam passes</li>
          <li>All required identity, residency, and supporting documents (scanned and certified where required)</li>
          <li>Payment of the application fee (€100–€150)</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>After submission:</strong> You receive a submission confirmation and reference number. Keep this number for all future communications. The Ministry will review your file and contact you if additional information is needed.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 6: Wait for Resolution + Oath Ceremony -->
      <div style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
        <h3>Step 6: Wait for Resolution and Attend Oath Ceremony</h3>
        <p>Once submitted, your application enters a review queue. Processing times vary widely due to backlog but typically range from 1 to 3 years. You can check the status of your application at any time on the Ministry portal using your reference number.</p>
        <p>When your application is <strong>approved</strong>, you will receive official notice from the Ministry. You must then attend an oath ceremony (jura o promesa) at your local Civil Registry (Registro Civil) office within the specified timeframe (usually 30 days). See <strong>After Approval — The Oath Ceremony</strong> below for details.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- DOCUMENTS YOU'LL NEED -->
  <div class="msv__section--cream">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Documents You'll Need</h2>
        <p>Accurate, certified documents are essential. Most must be original documents or official certified copies. Non-Spanish documents typically require a certified translation into Spanish.</p>
      </div>

      <h3>Identity & Personal Documents</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Valid passport or national ID from your home country</li>
        <li>Spanish residence card (TIE) or residence certificate from the National Police (Policía Nacional)</li>
        <li>Birth certificate (original or certified copy, officially translated into Spanish)</li>
        <li>Marriage or partnership certificate (if applicable, translated and certified)</li>
        <li>Divorce or separation decree (if applicable, translated and certified)</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Residency Documentation</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Continuous residency certificate from the National Police (Certificado de Antecedentes de Extranjería)</li>
        <li>Tax identification number (NIF) registration proof</li>
        <li>Social Security registration and contribution history (extract from your contribution account)</li>
        <li>Utility bills or rental contracts proving continuous residence in Spain (typically 2–3 years of recent examples)</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Exam Certifications</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Official DELE A2 exam results (issued by Cervantes Institute)</li>
        <li>Official CCSE exam results (issued by Spanish Administration)</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Conduct & Criminal Record</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Certificate of good conduct (Certificado de Antecedentes Penales) from Spain</li>
        <li>Certificate of good conduct from your country of origin or residency during the past 5 years (if applicable)</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Supporting Documents</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Complete curriculum vitae (CV)</li>
        <li>Letter of intent or personal statement (explaining your commitment to Spain and Spanish citizenship)</li>
        <li>Copies of any professional qualifications or certifications held in Spain</li>
      </ul>

      <p style="margin-top: 2rem; padding: 1.2rem; background: #f9fafb; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid var(--red);"><strong>Translation Tip:</strong> All non-Spanish documents must be officially translated. Use a certified translator (traductor jurado) listed in the Official Register at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs. Do not use informal or machine translations.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

