      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>After Approval — The Oath Ceremony (Jura de la Constitución)</h2>
        <p>Approval of your citizenship application is only the first part of the process. You must complete one final, essential step: taking the oath of allegiance to the Spanish Constitution and the King.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__two-col">
        <div>
          <h3>What Is the Oath Ceremony?</h3>
          <p>The <strong>jura de la Constitución</strong> (oath of the Constitution) or <strong>promesa de fidelidad</strong> (promise of loyalty) is a formal public ceremony held at your local Civil Registry office. In front of the registrar and witnesses, you solemnly pledge your allegiance to:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>His Majesty the King of Spain</li>
            <li>The Spanish Constitution and democratic values</li>
            <li>The laws of Spain</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The ceremony typically lasts 15–30 minutes and is conducted in Spanish. It is a ceremonial but legally binding act—only after taking the oath does your Spanish citizenship become official.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__two-col-card">
          <div class="msv__two-col-icon">📋</div>
          <h3>Timeline & Logistics</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Notification:</strong> After approval, you receive official notice from the Ministry specifying the deadline to schedule your oath ceremony (usually 30 days).</li>
            <li><strong>Scheduling:</strong> Contact your local Civil Registry to schedule a specific date and time.</li>
            <li><strong>Duration:</strong> Plan for 30–45 minutes including paperwork and formalities.</li>
            <li><strong>Attendance:</strong> You must attend in person. Proxies or remote attendance is not permitted.</li>
            <li><strong>Witnesses:</strong> Bring 2–3 witnesses (can be friends, family, or colleagues, must be 18+).</li>
            <li><strong>Documentation:</strong> Bring your valid passport and ID, and the approval notice from the Ministry.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>After the Oath: Getting Your Citizenship Certificate</h3>
      <p>Immediately after taking the oath, the Civil Registry issues your <strong>Certificado de Nacionalidad</strong> (Certificate of Spanish Nationality). This official document proves you are a Spanish citizen. You should:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Request certified copies (at least 3–5 copies recommended)</li>
        <li>Store one copy in a safe place and keep one with your important documents</li>
        <li>Use this certificate to apply for a Spanish passport (if desired) at any National Police office</li>
        <li>Use it as proof of citizenship for employment, banking, voting registration, or other purposes</li>
      </ul>

      <p><strong>Spanish Passport:</strong> While a citizenship certificate is your official proof of nationality, many people then apply for a Spanish passport (Pasaporte Español). This provides a travel document and further legal protection. Passports are valid for 10 years (for adults 30+ years old) and can be obtained at your nearest National Police office.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

