        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>How long does it take to get Spanish citizenship?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Processing times typically range from 1 to 3 years from submission to final decision. However, due to significant application backlogs at the Ministry of Justice, your specific case may take longer. It's recommended to apply as soon as you meet all eligibility requirements. The process cannot be expedited, so starting early is your best strategy.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Can I apply for Spanish citizenship online?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes, applications are submitted online through the official Ministry of Justice portal at sede.mjusticia.gob.es. You'll need a digital certificate or Spanish electronic ID (DNI electrónico) to authenticate your submission. All supporting documents are uploaded as scans. You cannot submit a paper application.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What is the DELE A2 language test?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>DELE A2 (Diploma of Spanish as a Foreign Language, Level A2) is an official Spanish language proficiency exam recognized by the Spanish Ministry of Education. It tests basic ability to understand and communicate in Spanish across reading, writing, listening, and speaking. You must pass this test to proceed with your citizenship application. The exam costs €120–€150 and is offered multiple times per year.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What is the CCSE culture test?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>The CCSE (Constitutional and Civic Knowledge of Spain) test assesses your understanding of Spanish law, government, history, and culture. You answer 25 multiple-choice questions and must answer at least 15 correctly to pass. The test is conducted in Spanish and typically takes 45 minutes. It costs approximately €30 and is designed to ensure applicants understand Spanish constitutional values and democratic principles.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>How much does Spanish citizenship cost?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Total costs typically range from €300–€400 depending on exam locations and any legal assistance. This breaks down as: DELE A2 exam (~€120–€150), CCSE test (~€25–€35), application fee (~€100–€150), and optional legal fees (€500–€1,500). Additional costs may include certified translations (€50–€200) and certified document copies (€2–€5 each).</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What happens after my citizenship application is approved?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>After approval, you must attend an oath ceremony (jura/promesa) at your local Civil Registry office within a specified timeframe (usually 30 days). You'll swear allegiance to the King and the Spanish Constitution. Only after taking the oath does Spanish citizenship become official. You then receive a Certificate of Spanish Nationality (Certificado de Nacionalidad), which proves your citizenship status.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Can I speed up the citizenship application process?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>The standard application process cannot be expedited through any official channel. However, ensuring all documents are complete, correctly certified, and accurately submitted can prevent delays and requests for additional information. Having a legal professional review your application before submission reduces the risk of errors that might trigger further communications from the Ministry.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What if my citizenship application is denied?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>If your application is denied, you have the right to appeal the decision within the specified timeframe. Grounds for denial typically include failing to meet residency requirements, failing language or culture tests, providing false information, or demonstrating conduct contrary to Spanish values. Consult with a legal professional to understand the specific reason for denial and your options for appeal or reapplication.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

